- op: create  # c74a
  at: '2026-08-30T08:41:55Z'  # c74a
  by: agent  # c74a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c74a
  clock: 1  # c74a
  nonce: c74a  # c74a
  data:  # c74a
    kind: invariant  # c74a
    title: "Unconditional stdin reads are absent or declared fs_read in five languages, and fs_read mints no taint source, so the canonical untrusted-input call is undetectable"  # c74a
    status: violated  # c74a
    priority: 1  # c74a
    parent:  # c74a
    tags:  # c74a
    - catalogue  # c74a
    - io_boundary  # c74a
    - taint  # c74a
    - analysis_quality  # c74a
    isbefore: []  # c74a
    duplicate_of: []  # c74a
    not_duplicate_of: []  # c74a
    pr_ref:  # c74a
    description: "FOUND 2026-08-30 while doing WI-vutav, by sweeping the mechanism across every\ncatalogue rather than only go's (LIVE rule 5). WI-vutav asked \"catalogue the read\ncalls behind go's stdin handles\"; the sweep says the gap is a cross-language\nfamily and the direction is the expensive one.\n\nTHE OBSERVATION. A read that is UNCONDITIONALLY from standard input needs no\nargument and no receiver typing -- it is the easy case, and it is the one the\ncatalogues get wrong. Swept over all 15 io_primitives catalogues:\n\n  go       fmt.{Scan,Scanln,Scanf}                 ABSENT ENTIRELY\n  python   builtins.input                          ABSENT (and python.yaml's\n                                                   ambiguous_names comment\n                                                   asserts \"not IO boundary\")\n  c        stdio.{getchar,scanf}                   declared fs_read\n  haskell  Prelude.{getContents,readLn,interact}    declared fs_read\n  scala    scala.io.StdIn.{readLine,readInt,readDouble,readFloat,readLong,\n           readBoolean,readChar,readShort,readByte,readf}   declared fs_read,\n           under a note that says \"Scala stdlib stdin reading\"\n\nPOSITIVE CONTROLS, same family, same shape, classified CORRECTLY -- so this is\ndrift and not an unmade decision:\n  cpp      std.cin                                 ipc_recv\n  rust     std::io::Stdin.{read_line,lines,read_to_string}   ipc_recv\n  c        stdio.stdin (the HANDLE)                ipc_recv\n\nTHE DIRECTION IS A SILENT FALSE NEGATIVE. AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP is\n{env_read: host_secret, host_info_read: host_description,\n net_recv|ipc_recv|db_read: untrusted_input}. fs_read IS NOT IN IT. So a call\nclassified fs_read mints NO taint source at all -- not a weaker one, none. A C\nprogram doing `scanf(\"%s\", buf); system(buf);` produces nothing; so does a Scala\nprogram doing `val s = StdIn.readLine(); Process(s).run()`. The canonical\nuntrusted-input example in three of these languages is undetectable.\n\nNOTE THE SHAPE RELATIVE TO THE ADR-0049 CAMPAIGN. That campaign removes rows that\nmint a source where no data crosses (false sources). This is its MIRROR: the call\nwhere data genuinely crosses mints nothing. Both are INV-nular's rule -- kind\nasserted by name, never checked against semantics -- and the two directions have\nto be counted separately or the campaign will report progress while the recall\nhole grows.\n\nWHAT IS AND IS NOT IN SCOPE. IN: reads with no stream argument, where stdin is\nthe only thing the call can read. OUT: the argument-conditioned reads\n(c stdio.{fgets,fscanf,fread,getc}, java.util.Scanner.*, go bufio.Reader.*,\nhaskell System.IO.hGet*), which are INV-zumin class (b) -- undecidable at the\ncall site -- and are INV-bagok's subject, not this one. Keeping the two apart is\nthe whole point: the unconditional set is a clean fix, the conditional set needs\ninference.\n\nPYTHON HAS A SECOND HALF AND IT MAY BE FREE. INV-foluz records that python emits\nno call edge at all for a bare `input()`, because py.py's bare-Name arm emits an\nedge only for names in BUILTIN_CONSTRUCTOR_NAMES -- a set DERIVED FROM THE\nio_primitives CATALOGUE. If that derivation is what it says it is, cataloguing\nbuiltins.input as ipc_recv admits the edge as a side effect and closes both\nhalves with one row. VERIFY BEFORE CLAIMING IT -- do not assume the derivation\ncovers this arm.\n\nREPRO\n  ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/vutav_reads_08302026/  (the sweep)\n  python3 -c \"from hypergumbo_core.taint import AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP; print(AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP)\""  # c74a
    fields:  # c74a
      statement: "A call that can only read standard input must carry a boundary AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP maps to untrusted_input. VIOLATED: go fmt.Scan* and python builtins.input are absent from the catalogues entirely; c stdio.getchar/scanf, haskell Prelude.getContents/readLn/interact and all ten scala.io.StdIn readers are declared fs_read, which is not in AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP, so they mint nothing. cpp std.cin and rust std::io::Stdin.read_line are the positive controls at ipc_recv."  # c74a
      root_cause: "The boundary was assigned from the call's SHAPE (it reads characters, so filesystem) rather than from the CHANNEL it can reach, and no gate compares a row's boundary against the stream its module names -- scala's row carries the note 'Scala stdlib stdin reading' beside a fs_read boundary. INV-nular's rule (kind asserted by name, never checked against semantics) in the recall direction rather than the precision direction the ADR-0049 campaign has been sweeping."  # c74a
- op: discuss  # b70c
  at: '2026-08-30T08:52:31Z'  # b70c
  by: agent  # b70c
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b70c
  clock: 2  # b70c
  nonce: b70c  # b70c
  message: "THE PYTHON ARM NEEDS TWO CHANGES, NOT ONE. This item's description floated the\npossibility that cataloguing builtins.input as ipc_recv would also admit the\nmissing call edge, because INV-foluz's root_cause describes\nBUILTIN_CONSTRUCTOR_NAMES as derived from the io_primitives catalogue. Checked in\ncode: py.py:568 is\n\n    BUILTIN_CONSTRUCTOR_NAMES: frozenset[str] = frozenset({\"open\"})\n\n-- a hardcoded literal that never consults the catalogue. So the python arm is a\ncatalogue row AND a name added to that set, and the row alone would be present\nand dead. The description's instruction to verify before claiming it stands\nsatisfied and negative; do not re-derive it.\n\nThe other four languages are unaffected by this -- go/c/haskell/scala emit the\ncall edge already and only the boundary is wrong or missing."  # b70c
- op: discuss  # e763
  at: '2026-08-30T09:49:07Z'  # e763
  by: agent  # e763
  actor: jgstern_agent  # e763
  clock: 3  # e763
  nonce: e763  # e763
  message: "SHIPPED, AND THE RECALL HALF IS SMALLER THAN THE CORRECTNESS HALF. All sixteen\nrows are now right; only two of the five languages can actually reach them\ntoday. Measured per language on fixtures rather than assumed\n(~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/vutav_reads_08302026/reach/):\n\n  c        scanf, getchar            FIRES  -> ipc_recv\n  go       fmt.Scan                  FIRES  -> ipc_recv\n  scala    scala.io.StdIn.readLine   correct, NOT REACHED: the analyzer emits\n           scala:external:0-0:readLine and readLine is in scala's\n           ambiguous_names, so the no-module gate withholds it. UNCHANGED by\n           this retag -- it was equally unreachable as fs_read.\n  haskell  Prelude.getLine           correct, NO CALL EDGE AT ALL for\n           \"name <- getLine\" in a do-block bind.\n  python   builtins.input            correct, NO CALL EDGE. INV-foluz, now\n           confirmed on a run instead of inferred.\n\nSo this is a TWO-language recall fix and a FIVE-language correctness fix, and\nthe two numbers must not be merged. A right-but-unreachable row becomes useful\nthe day its analyzer is fixed; a wrong one stays wrong. test_stdin_reads_reach_\nthe_catalogue.py pins only the two live arms -- pinning the three dead ones\nwould pin a bug in place.\n\nCORPUS EFFECT IS ZERO AND THE INSTRUMENT WAS CONTROLLED FIRST. Six repositories\nre-classified under both catalogues in one process, analyzer run once\n(shellcheck, tmate, tmux, caddy, jaeger, hypergumbo): not one boundary tag\nchanges. A uniform zero across six repos is the signature of a broken probe, so\nthe before-arm was positive-controlled -- it returns fs_read for\nscala.io.StdIn.readLine where the after-arm returns ipc_recv. The population\nreally is that thin: word-boundary greps find ONE genuine getLine in shellcheck\nand ZERO getchar/scanf/gets in tmux, against 28 substring \"hits\" that are\nsscanf, fgets and friends. Both numbers are recorded so the loose one cannot be\nquoted alone. The corpus is go/js/rust-heavy; a C or Scala CLI cohort would be\nthe honest place to look for effect.\n\nWHAT WAS DONE, exactly:\n  go       + ipc_recv fmt.{Scan,Scanln,Scanf}; + \"Scan\" to ambiguous_names,\n             which is NOT incidental -- without it every untyped sc.Scan() on a\n             bufio.Scanner picks up a stdin read through the short-name\n             fallback, manufacturing the false sources this item is the mirror\n             of. Fscan/Fscanf (take a reader) and Sscan (takes a string) are\n             deliberately absent.\n  python   + ipc_recv builtins.input; ambiguous_names comment corrected -- it\n             asserted \"(not IO boundary)\", which was wrong.\n  c        getchar/scanf MOVED fs_read -> ipc_recv, + gets. fgets/fgetc/getc/\n             fscanf/fread stay fs_read beside unistd.read, which already carries\n             boundary_ruling: call_site_undecidable for the same reason.\n  haskell  Prelude.{getContents,readLn,interact} MOVED; + getLine, getChar.\n             Data.Text.IO.{getContents,getLine} MOVED. System.IO's h* family\n             stays -- it takes a Handle.\n  scala    the whole scala.io.StdIn row MOVED fs_read -> ipc_recv.\n\nNOT DONE, ON A RULING: haskell Prelude.readIO stays fs_read. Its type is\nString -> IO a, so it crosses nothing and BOTH the row and the boundary are\nwrong -- INV-zumin class (d), which that item says needs its own item rather\nthan being absorbed by a neighbouring fix. Filed as INV-savuk.\n\nSTATUS: leaving this violated rather than satisfied. The rows are correct, but\nthe item's statement is about a call MINTING untrusted input, and in three of\nfive languages no call edge reaches the row at all -- so the behaviour the\nstatement describes still does not happen there. Closure-evidence discipline:\nthe behavioural evidence supports c and go only."  # e763
